by yceruto at 2025-06-04T14:32:33Z

@seb-jean Thank you for raising this topic.

>Have you also tried to see if the User entity is persisted in the database once the FormFlow is finished?

The root object is a DTO in my case, and that’s what I’d recommend most of the time. So, in the controller, when the FormFlow is finished, I can persist/update all related entities without any problem.
```php
$user = User::newFromDto($flow->getData());

$this->em->persist($user);
$this->em->flush();
```

If the root object is an entity instead (one that hasn’t been persisted yet) it will work the same as a DTO approach:
```php
$this->em->persist($flow->getData());
$this->em->flush();
```
In both cases above, you might need to reload the related entities, if there are any.

However, when it’s a managed entity (one that has already been persisted), and the FormFlow is finished, you’ll need to use that managed entity and map/update all fields from `$flow->getData()`. Otherwise, you’ll end up ignoring the changes or creating a new record in the database (if `persist()` is used) instead of updating the existing one:
```php
$user->updateFrom($flow->getData());

$this->em->flush();
```

Another alternative is to create a `DoctrineDataStorage` (it might be introduced later in a different PR) that handles persisting and flushing changes across steps ([here is a sample](https://gist.github.com/yceruto/4be95e605934311f066b8171c3503167)), instead of the default `SessionDataStorage`. Thus, your root entity (along with its related entities) will always be in a "managed" state.

